-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Whole-Visit Revision Workflow
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+For a new visit, the store accepts the complete visit payload as its first revision. For an edit, the submission includes the visit ID, the base revision ID, the complete replacement payload, and an idempotency key.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+When the base revision is the visit's selected revision and the visit has no unresolved review work, the store accepts the submission as the next selected revision. The earlier revision remains in history.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+When an edit names an older base revision, the store accepts its complete payload as a competing revision and records unresolved review work for the visit. It does not merge individual fields or replace the selected revision based on arrival time. A coordinator resolves the review work by selecting one competing complete revision as the visit's selected revision and recording the resolution with the coordinator identity and the contributing revision IDs.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Downstream Contracts
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Tablet-sync must retain the revision ID used as the base for each submitted edit, retry that submission with the same idempotency key, and show when a submitted visit is awaiting coordinator resolution. It sends complete visit payloads; it does not compute or submit field-level merge instructions.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Review-export must compare complete competing revisions, identify the revision selected by the coordinator, and exclude every visit with unresolved review work from the reviewed CSV. It must retain enough revision and resolution provenance to explain an exported visit's selected state.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions with coordinator selection | Field-aware three-way merge | The team prefers a larger explicit review queue to automatic combination rules that could separate scientifically related edits. Whole revisions preserve each worker's submitted observation as a reviewable unit and avoid treating independent-looking fields as scientifically independent. |
+
+Field-aware three-way merge remains a credible future approach if review volume becomes unsustainable and the team can define and maintain scientifically sound merge rules for every visit field. It could automatically combine truly unrelated changes and reduce excluded visits, but would require base snapshots, structured changes, field-level provenance, and explicit handling for relationships such as species identification and cover estimates. It is not part of this release's record-store contract.
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. Concurrent edits are retained as whole competing revisions and resolved by a coordinator rather than automatically merged.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a coordinator may create a new corrected revision while resolving a conflict, rather than selecting an existing competing revision.
+2. How a later submission is associated with an already unresolved visit.
